but the documentation indicates magefiles is provided by cobbler-scaffold's orchestrator library and shouldn't be modified unless working on srd011. Since srd011 is in requirement_states with ready items, I should check if those requirements are actually actionable or if the provided nature of magefiles means I should skip them.\n\nLooking at the generation pipeline, I realize the stitch agent will build cmd/ packages sequentially, and each one depends on pkg/testutils for differential testing. Even if pkg/testutils doesn't exist yet, the code generation can still proceed—the tests might fail initially, but that's acceptable since the task is to create the code itself. Let me reconsider which requirements are actually ready to propose as tasks based on their dependencies and the order of generation. I'm realizing that cmd/ utilities using differential testing absolutely need testutils to compile, but cmd/version is simpler since it just prints version info without comparing against a GNU binary. Let me focus on what's actually proposable right now—cmd/version seems like the cleanest starting point since it doesn't have those external dependencies blocking it.\n\nLooking at srd059-version, all five requirements are ready, but splitting them across tasks gets awkward with the 4-requirement limit. Since cmd/version is probably straightforward to implement, I should check if the requirements have different weights before deciding whether to split it into multiple tasks.\n\nI'm realizing that most cmd/ utilities depend on pkg/testutils for differential testing against the GNU reference binary, which means they can't proceed until testutils is available. This is a hard blocker for many of the later releases, even if their individual requirements are marked ready.\n\nThe exception is cmd/version, which doesn't have a GNU equivalent to test against, so I could propose those tasks independently. For the truly trivial utilities like true and false, I could potentially propose