cus on what makes sense practically: Task 0 covers the main cmd/version functionality, Task 1 handles the remaining version requirement, and Tasks 2-3 tackle the trivial utilities from rel02.0 that don't require testutils for implementation. I need to structure the task descriptions so the stitch agent knows exactly which SRD files to read and which requirements to implement, rather than trying to figure out the specs myself. Let me organize the first task with the SRD reference, the files to create or modify, and the specific requirement IDs it needs to handle. I need to make sure each task is self-contained so the agent can execute it without additional context. Since I don't have access to the full SRD content, I should write task descriptions that reference the specific SRD document and tell the agent which requirements to implement, letting them read the details themselves. Looking at what's available, the simpler utilities with ready requirements seem like good candidates to start with—things like the true and false commands with their basic exit codes and help/version flags, or the yes command that outputs repeated strings.\n\nNow I'm scanning through the other utilities to find ones with manageable requirement counts. The arch, hostid, whoami, hostname, and logname commands all have around 7-8 requirements each, while version is even simpler at just 5. These would be good starting points since they're straightforward to implement and don't have complex dependencies. I'm thinking about how to distribute these across releases, starting with the simplest ones first—version with its 5 requirements could be a good initial release, followed by the true command with its 11 requirements in a later phase. Looking at the dependencies more carefully, cat needs testutils for testing but I can still propose the implementation without tests. Since rel00.0 is blocked by infrastructure I can't build, I'll focus on proposing implementation-only tasks starting with cmd/versio