AML list, but I need to think through what the stitch agent can actually accomplish—without pkg/testutils available, I should reconsider what's feasible for the test file creation. propose implementation tasks (main.go), not test tasks (cat_test.go).\n\nBut the constraint says \"Always split implementation and tests into separate tasks.\" Since I'm not proposing test tasks (they'd need testutils which isn't available), I just need implementation tasks. That's fine.\n\nFor cmd/version, the stitch agent needs:\n- SRD059 for requirements\n- Knowledge of how to create a cmd/version/main.go\n\nFor cmd/cat, the stitch agent needs:\n- SRD006 for requirements\n- pkg/sys for SIGPIPE handler\n- Understanding of differential testing pattern (but we won't create tests yet)\n\nNow I'm drafting the task descriptions. I need to be specific about what each requirement covers, even though I'm pointing to the SRD. The task requirements should describe what to implement, not just \"implement R1.1.\" Since I don't have the SRD content, I'll describe the expected behavior based on the utility's well-known functionality and the requirement group structure.\n\nFor cmd/version, R1.1-R1.4 likely cover: basic version output, flag parsing, help text, and error handling. For cmd/cat, R1.1-R1.4 likely cover: reading files, handling stdin, concatenating multiple files, and error handling.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what the stitch agent actually needs to know. The task description should specify which SRD sections to read, what files to create or modify, and the concrete requirements—since the agent has access to the project rules but needs explicit guidance on scope and deliverables.\n\nFor the version command, I'd point to srd059 and have it implement the core requirements that produce version output. For cat, I'd reference srd006 and break the implementation across three tasks to handle file concatenation, stdin, and display flags progressively.\n\nThe tricky part is verification without tes