ent IDs are actually listed in the requirement_states rather than guessing at them.\n\nFor the cmd/cat task, I'm inferring what each requirement likely means based on typical cat functionality, but the stitch agent will validate against the actual SRD. So my task requirements should explicitly reference the SRD items like \"Implement srd006-cat R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.4 as specified in the SRD\" and let the agent pull the full details from there.\n\nLooking at the example pattern, the requirement text just needs to reference the SRD requirement ID, and the stitch agent handles reading the actual spec to get the details. Now I'll write out the final task structure following that approach. Now I'm looking at the next task: implementing a basic cat command that reads files and handles stdin, following the same patterns I just established for the version command. file concatenation in argument order\n      - id: R4\n        text: Implement srd006-cat R1.4 -- exit status on success\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at start of main per project SIGPIPE protocol\n      - id: D2\n        text: Binary lives in cmd/cat/ and builds with go build -o bin/cat ./cmd/cat/\n      - id: D3\n        text: Structure main.go to support incremental addition of display flags in subsequent tasks\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: go build -o bin/cat ./cmd/cat/ succeeds\n      - id: AC2\n        text: go vet ./cmd/cat/ passes with no warnings\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"echo hello | bin/cat outputs hello to stdout\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: bin/cat reads and concatenates multiple named files to stdout\n\nNow I'm looking at the next task which focuses on error handling and line numbering features, building on the basic cat implementation with additional flags for R1.5 and the R2 requirements. Looking at the acceptance criteria for the cat implementation, I need to verify that line numbering works correc